+## Pitfalls
+
+1. **Placeholder text in output** — Templates often contain "Lorem ipsum" or "XXXX" placeholders. Always grep the output with `python -m markitdown output.pptx | grep -iE "xxxx|lorem|ipsum|this.*(page|slide).*layout"` before declaring success.
+
+2. **Text overflow at box boundaries** — Text boxes with narrow widths cause excessive wrapping or cut-off text. Verify with visual QA (convert to images) before delivery.
+
+3. **Overlapping elements** — Decorative lines positioned for single-line titles break when titles wrap to two lines. Always check for elements that cross text boundaries.
+
+4. **Accent lines under titles** — Horizontal accent lines under slide titles are a hallmark of AI-generated slides. Use whitespace or background color blocks instead.
+
+5. **Low-contrast text and icons** — Light gray text on cream backgrounds or dark icons on dark backgrounds without contrasting circles are unreadable. Test contrast ratios for every element.
+
+6. **Inconsistent spacing** — Mixing 0.3" and 0.5" gaps randomly across slides looks unprofessional. Pick one spacing system and apply it consistently.
+
+7. **Leftover template content after editing** — When editing existing decks, old footers, source citations, or date fields from the original often collide with new content. Audit every slide for orphaned template elements.
+
+8. **Forgetting text box padding** — When aligning lines or shapes with text edges, the text box has internal padding that offsets the apparent edge. Set `margin: 0` on the text box or offset the shape to account for padding.
+
+9. **Same layout on every slide** — Repeating identical two-column or bullet layouts across all slides is visually boring. Vary layouts: two-column, icon rows, 2×2 grid, half-bleed image, stat callouts.
+
+10. **Centered body text** — Center-aligning paragraphs and lists reduces readability. Left-align body text; center only slide titles.
+
+11. **Missing font on target machine** — Using niche fonts (e.g., "Palatino", "Garamond") causes fallback to Calibri on machines without them. Stick to universally available fonts or embed fonts in the .pptx.
+
+12. **QA without image conversion** — You cannot reliably judge slide layout from the code alone. Always convert to images (`soffice --convert-to pdf` then `pdftoppm`) and visually inspect before declaring success.
+
